<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baybars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1260">1260</a>–<a href="/years/1277">1277</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Baybars I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baybars, or Baybars I, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> who reigned from 1260 to 1277. As a general he shared in the victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260, before seizing the throne. His reign established the military credibility of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> by repelling Mongol incursions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and capturing many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> strongholds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baybars.jpg" alt="Baybars" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 14th century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baybars,_Medallion_IV,_Baptist%C3%A8re_de_Saint-Louis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
